\textbf{(a) [2023 Q1(xii)]} A car number plate is formed by 4 digits
from $\{1,2,\ldots,9\}$ followed by 3 letters from the alphabet (26
letters). Neither digits nor letters are repeated. How many plates
are possible?

\textbf{(b)} How many bit strings of length 8 are there?

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.2cm}
\textbf{(a)} Seven successive slots, no repetition within digits or
within letters:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
Digit 1 & Digit 2 & Digit 3 & Digit 4 & Letter 1 & Letter 2 & Letter 3 \\
\hline
9 & 8 & 7 & 6 & 26 & 25 & 24 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\[
    9 \times 8 \times 7 \times 6 \times 26 \times 25 \times 24
    = 3024 \times 15600 = \boxed{47{,}174{,}400}
\]

\vspace{0.2cm}
\textbf{(b)} Each of the 8 bits is independently 0 or 1 (2 choices):
\[
    \underbrace{2 \times 2 \times \cdots \times 2}_{8} = 2^8 = \boxed{256}
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}

How many license plates can be made using either:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
    \item two uppercase English letters followed by four digits, \textbf{or}
    \item two digits followed by four uppercase English letters?
\end{itemize}
Repetition is allowed.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.2cm}
The two formats are \textbf{mutually exclusive} (different structures).

\textbf{Case 1 --- 2 letters then 4 digits:}
\[
    \underbrace{26 \times 26}_{\text{letters}} \times
    \underbrace{10 \times 10 \times 10 \times 10}_{\text{digits}}
    = 26^2 \times 10^4 = 676 \times 10{,}000 = 6{,}760{,}000
\]

\textbf{Case 2 --- 2 digits then 4 letters:}
\[
    \underbrace{10 \times 10}_{\text{digits}} \times
    \underbrace{26 \times 26 \times 26 \times 26}_{\text{letters}}
    = 10^2 \times 26^4 = 100 \times 456{,}976 = 45{,}697{,}600
\]

\textbf{By Sum Rule} (two disjoint cases):
\[
    6{,}760{,}000 + 45{,}697{,}600 = \boxed{52{,}457{,}600}
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}

If a task can be done in $N$ total ways and $n_{\text{bad}}$ of those
ways violate a constraint, then the number of \textbf{valid} ways is:
\[
    N - n_{\text{bad}}
\]

This is called \textbf{complementary counting}: it is often easier to
count what you do \textbf{not} want and subtract.

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faInfoCircle\ When to use:}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item Keywords: \textbf{``at least one''}, \textbf{``not all''},
          \textbf{``except''}, \textbf{``other than''}.
    \item When counting valid outcomes directly is harder than counting
          invalid ones.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

If a counting method counts each distinct object exactly $d$ times,
the number of distinct objects is:
\[
    \frac{\text{total count}}{d}
\]

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faInfoCircle\ Key application:}}
The \textbf{Handshaking Lemma} is a direct application. Each edge is
counted twice in the degree sum (once for each endpoint):
\[
    \sum_{v \in V} \deg(v) = 2|E|
    \quad \Rightarrow \quad
    |E| = \frac{\sum \deg(v)}{2}
\]

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{accentorange}{\textbf{Past Paper:}} 2021 Q1(vii) ---
``How many edges in a graph with 10 vertices each of degree 6?''

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textbf{(a)} How many 3-digit numbers can be formed from
$\{1,2,3,4,5\}$ with no repeated digits? How many of these are even?

\textbf{(b)} A committee of 2 is chosen from 4 men and 3 women. How
many committees have \textbf{at least one woman}?

\textbf{(c)} How many 4-character passwords use letters A--Z and digits
0--9 and contain \textbf{at least one digit}?

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.2cm}
\textbf{(a)} No repetition, 3 digits from 5:
\[
    5 \times 4 \times 3 = \boxed{60}
\]
Even means last digit $\in \{2,4\}$ (2 choices).
Fix last digit (2 choices), then first two slots get 4 and 3 choices:
\[
    4 \times 3 \times 2 = \boxed{24}
\]

\textbf{(b)} Total committees of 2 from 7:
$\binom{7}{2} = 21$.

All-male committees: $\binom{4}{2} = 6$.

At least one woman:
\[
    21 - 6 = \boxed{15}
\]

\textbf{(c)} Each position: 26 + 10 = 36 choices.
Total $= 36^4 = 1{,}679{,}616$.

No digit (all letters): $26^4 = 456{,}976$.

At least one digit:
\[
    1{,}679{,}616 - 456{,}976 = \boxed{1{,}222{,}640}
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}




\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[
