A \textbf{sequence} is a function from a subset of the integers
(usually $\{0,1,2,\ldots\}$ or $\{1,2,3,\ldots\}$) to a set $S$.

We write the sequence as $\{a_n\}$ and call $a_n$ the
\textbf{$n$th term}.

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faInfoCircle\ Common sequence types:}}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|l|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Type} & \textbf{Formula} & \textbf{First terms} \\
\hline
Explicit (closed form) & $a_n = f(n)$ & $a_n=2^{n-1}$: 1,2,4,8,16\ldots \\
\hline
Arithmetic & $a_n = a_0 + nd$ & $d=3,\,a_0=2$: 2,5,8,11\ldots \\
\hline
Geometric & $a_n = a_0 \cdot r^n$ & $r=3,\,a_0=2$: 2,6,18,54\ldots \\
\hline
Recurrence & $a_n$ from earlier terms & $F_n=F_{n-1}+F_{n-2}$: 1,1,2,3,5\ldots \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{accentorange}{\textbf{Past Paper:}} 2020 Q1(viii), 2022 Q1(xi)

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textbf{(a) [2020 Q1(viii)]} What is $a_8$ of the sequence $\{a_n\}$
where $a_n = 2^{n-1}$?

\textbf{(b) [2022 Q1(xi)]} What are $a_1$ and $a_3$ of the sequence
$\{a_n\}$ where $a_n = \lfloor n/2 \rfloor + \lceil n/2 \rceil$?

\textbf{(c)} Find the first five terms of $a_n = 3n-2$ and
$b_n = (-1)^n \cdot n$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.2cm}
\textbf{(a)} Substitute $n=8$:
\[
    a_8 = 2^{8-1} = 2^7 = \boxed{128}
\]

\textbf{(b)} $\lfloor n/2 \rfloor + \lceil n/2 \rceil = n$ for all
positive integers (floor and ceiling of $n/2$ always sum to $n$).

$a_1 = \lfloor 1/2 \rfloor + \lceil 1/2 \rceil = 0 + 1 = \boxed{1}$

$a_3 = \lfloor 3/2 \rfloor + \lceil 3/2 \rceil = 1 + 2 = \boxed{3}$

\textbf{(c)}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
$n$ & 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 \\
\hline
$a_n = 3n-2$ & 1 & 4 & 7 & 10 & 13 \\
\hline
$b_n = (-1)^n n$ & $-1$ & $2$ & $-3$ & $4$ & $-5$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.5cm}

A \textbf{recurrence relation} for $\{a_n\}$ is an equation that
expresses $a_n$ in terms of one or more previous terms
$a_{n-1}, a_{n-2}, \ldots$

Together with \textbf{initial conditions} (values for the first
terms), a recurrence relation \textbf{uniquely determines} the
entire sequence.

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textbf{Standard types and closed-form solutions:}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|l|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Recurrence} & \textbf{Type} & \textbf{Closed form} \\
\hline
$a_n = a_{n-1} + d$ & Arithmetic & $a_n = a_0 + nd$ \\
\hline
$a_n = r \cdot a_{n-1}$ & Geometric & $a_n = a_0 \cdot r^n$ \\
\hline
$a_n = a_{n-1} + a_{n-2}$ & Fibonacci & No simple closed form \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{accentorange}{\textbf{Past Paper:}} 2020 Q6(a)(b), 2023 Q1(xi)

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textbf{(a) [2020 Q6(a)]} Let $\{a_n\}$ satisfy
$a_n = a_{n-1} + 3$ for $n=1,2,3,\ldots$ with $a_0 = 2$.
Find $a_1$, $a_2$, $a_3$.

\textbf{(b) [2020 Q6(b)]} Solve $a_k = 3\,a_{k-1}$ for $k=1,2,3,\ldots$
with $a_0 = 2$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.2cm}
\textbf{(a)} Apply the recurrence step by step:

$a_1 = a_0 + 3 = 2 + 3 = \boxed{5}$

$a_2 = a_1 + 3 = 5 + 3 = \boxed{8}$

$a_3 = a_2 + 3 = 8 + 3 = \boxed{11}$

This is an \textbf{arithmetic sequence} with $d=3$.

Closed form: $a_n = a_0 + nd = 2 + 3n$.

Verify: $a_3 = 2+3(3) = 11$ \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark}

\vspace{0.2cm}
\textbf{(b)} This is a \textbf{geometric recurrence} with ratio $r=3$.

The general solution is:
\[
    a_k = a_0 \cdot r^k = 2 \cdot 3^k
\]

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
$k$ & 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\
\hline
$a_k = 2\cdot3^k$ & 2 & 6 & 18 & 54 & 162 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Verify: $a_1 = 3a_0 = 3\times2 = 6$ \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textbf{(a) [2020 Q1(xvi)]} Evaluate $\displaystyle\sum_{j=1}^{5} j^2$.

\textbf{(b)} Evaluate $\displaystyle\sum_{j=1}^{100} j$.

\textbf{(c)} Evaluate $\displaystyle\sum_{j=0}^{4} 3^j$.

\textbf{(d)} Evaluate $\displaystyle\sum_{j=1}^{5}(2j+1)$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.2cm}
\textbf{(a)} Formula with $n=5$:
\[
    \sum_{j=1}^{5} j^2
    = \frac{5\times6\times11}{6}
    = \frac{330}{6} = \boxed{55}
\]
Verify: $1+4+9+16+25=55$ \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark}

\textbf{(b)}
\[
    \sum_{j=1}^{100} j = \frac{100\times101}{2} = \boxed{5050}
\]

\textbf{(c)} Geometric series, $r=3$, indices $j=0$ to $4$:
\[
    \sum_{j=0}^{4} 3^j
    = \frac{3^5-1}{3-1}
    = \frac{242}{2} = \boxed{121}
\]
Verify: $1+3+9+27+81=121$ \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark}

\textbf{(d)} Split using linearity:
\[
    \sum_{j=1}^{5}(2j+1)
    = 2\sum_{j=1}^{5}j + \sum_{j=1}^{5}1
    = 2\cdot\frac{5\times6}{2} + 5
    = 30 + 5 = \boxed{35}
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}

A \textbf{recursive algorithm} solves a problem by reducing it to
\textbf{smaller instances of the same problem}.

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faInfoCircle\ Two required components:}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item \textbf{Base case:} simplest input, solved directly with no
          recursion.
    \item \textbf{Recursive case:} solution expressed in terms of the
          same algorithm on a smaller input.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Template:}
\begin{verbatim}
ALGORITHM name(input):
    if base case:
        return base value
    else:
        return f( name(smaller input) )
\end{verbatim}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Key insight:} Tracing a recursive call has two phases:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em]
    \item \textbf{Descent:} each call reduces the problem, printing or
          computing on the way \textit{down}.
    \item \textbf{Ascent:} as calls return, further actions happen on
          the way \textit{back up}.
\end{enumerate}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{accentorange}{\textbf{Past Paper:}} 2023 Q5 ---
``Write a recursive algorithm for PATTERN.''

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textbf{(a) [2023 Q1(xi)]} Define the recurrence relation for the sum
of the first 5 integers and evaluate it.

\textbf{(b)} Write a recurrence for $n!$ and compute $5!$.

\textbf{(c)} Find $F_3$ through $F_8$ for the Fibonacci sequence
$F_n = F_{n-1}+F_{n-2}$, $F_1=1$, $F_2=1$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.2cm}
\textbf{(a)} Let $S_n$ = sum of first $n$ positive integers.

Recurrence: $S_n = S_{n-1} + n$, \quad $S_1 = 1$.

$S_1=1,\quad S_2=3,\quad S_3=6,\quad S_4=10,\quad
S_5 = S_4+5 = 10+5 = \boxed{15}$

Closed form check: $\dfrac{5\times6}{2} = 15$
\textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark}

\vspace{0.2cm}
\textbf{(b)} Recurrence: $n! = n\times(n-1)!$, \quad $0!=1$.

$1!=1,\ 2!=2,\ 3!=6,\ 4!=24,\ 5!=5\times24=\boxed{120}$

\vspace{0.2cm}
\textbf{(c)}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
$n$ & 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 & 6 & 7 & 8 \\
\hline
$F_n$ & 1 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 5 & 8 & 13 & 21 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.5cm}

Write a recursive algorithm such that:

\textsc{Pattern}$(1,\,5)$ produces: $5,\;4,\;3,\;2,\;1,\;2,\;3,\;4,\;5$

\textsc{Pattern}$(17,\,20)$ produces: $20,\;19,\;18,\;17,\;18,\;19,\;20$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Analysis:}}

The output counts \textbf{down} from $high$ to $low$, then back
\textbf{up} from $low+1$ to $high$.

This is achieved by printing $high$ \textbf{before} the recursive
call (descent) and then printing $high$ \textbf{again after} the call
returns (ascent).

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Algorithm:}}

\begin{verbatim}
ALGORITHM Pattern(low, high):
    print high
    if high > low:
        Pattern(low, high - 1)
        print high
\end{verbatim}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Call trace for Pattern(1, 5):}}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Call} & \textbf{Output} \\
\hline
Pattern(1,5): print \textbf{5}; recurse $\to$ & 5 \\
\quad Pattern(1,4): print \textbf{4}; recurse $\to$ & 4 \\
\quad\quad Pattern(1,3): print \textbf{3}; recurse $\to$ & 3 \\
\quad\quad\quad Pattern(1,2): print \textbf{2}; recurse $\to$ & 2 \\
\quad\quad\quad\quad Pattern(1,1): print \textbf{1}; base case & 1 \\
\quad\quad\quad Pattern(1,2) returns: print \textbf{2} & 2 \\
\quad\quad Pattern(1,3) returns: print \textbf{3} & 3 \\
\quad Pattern(1,4) returns: print \textbf{4} & 4 \\
Pattern(1,5) returns: print \textbf{5} & 5 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Full output: $5,4,3,2,1,2,3,4,5$ \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Pattern(17,20) trace:} prints $20,19,18,17$ on descent;
$18,19,20$ on ascent. \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Recurrence for number of print statements:}

Let $T(n)$ where $n = high - low$.

$T(0) = 1$ (base: print once). $\quad T(n) = T(n-1)+2$.

Solution: $T(n) = 2n+1$.

Pattern(1,5): $n=4 \Rightarrow T(4)=9$ prints.
Count: $5,4,3,2,1,2,3,4,5$ = 9 \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark}

